from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('topic-22','analog','operational-amplifiers','Op-Amp Circuits · References, Dynamics & Limits','009');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'An ideal op-amp in stable linear operation holds its noninverting input at 2 V. Its inverting node connects to a 1 V source through 10 kΩ and to the output through 20 kΩ. Find output voltage in volts.',4,'Ideal input current zero; virtual equality at 2 V; resistive negative feedback','Solve the shifted-reference summing-node KCL.','Nonzero virtual reference',r'\frac{2-1}{10k}+\frac{2-V_o}{20k}=0',r'The inverting voltage equals the specified noninverting reference, 2 V. | Multiply node KCL by 20 kΩ: 2+(2−Vo)=0. | This gives Vo=4 V, assuming available supply range contains it.','Virtual equality does not imply virtual ground when the other input is nonzero.',unit='V')
b.n(r'An ideal integrator has output y(0)=1 V and obeys dy/dt=−100x(t), with x(t)=2 V for 0≤t≤0.01 s. Find y(0.01) in volts.',-1,'Initial output 1 V; constant slope −200 V/s over 10 ms','Include the integration constant.','Integrator state accumulation',r'y(t)=y(0)-100\int_0^t x(\tau)d\tau',r'The input area over the interval is 2×0.01=0.02 V·s. | The output change is −100×0.02=−2 V. | Add the initial 1 V to get y(0.01)=−1 V.','An integrator output includes its stored initial state.',unit='V')
b.n(r'An ideal inverting differentiator has R=10 kΩ,C=10 nF and input vi(t)=0.1sin(1000t) V in sinusoidal steady state. Find vo(0) in volts.',-0.01,'RC=10⁻⁴ s; smooth sinusoidal input defined through t=0','Find instantaneous output from the input derivative.','Differentiator phase and scale',r'v_o=-RC\,dv_i/dt',r'The derivative of the input is 100cos(1000t) V/s. | Multiplication by −RC=−10⁻⁴ s gives vo=−0.01cos(1000t) V. | At t=0, vo=−0.01 V.','Differentiate the sinusoid’s argument too; there is a factor 1000.',unit='V')
b.n(r'An inverting Schmitt trigger has output levels ±5 V. Its positive input is βvo with β=0.2 and input is at the negative terminal. Find the hysteresis width in volts.',2,'Zero-centered thresholds; β=0.2; ideal hard output levels ±5 V','Find upper threshold minus lower threshold.','Schmitt positive-feedback thresholds',r'V_{UT}=\beta V_{OH},\quad V_{LT}=\beta V_{OL}',r'At output +5 V, the comparison reference is +1 V. | At output −5 V, the reference is −1 V. | The two switching levels are separated by 1−(−1)=2 V.','Hysteresis width uses both output-dependent thresholds.',unit='V')
b.m(r'An ideal inverting amplifier has feedback Zf=Rf parallel with 1/(sC), and input resistor Rin. Its transfer is:','C',[r'$-sR_fC$',r'$-R_{in}/R_f$',r'$-(R_f/R_{in})/(1+sR_fC)$',r'$+(R_f/R_{in})(1+sR_fC)$'],'Linear op-amp; grounded noninverting input; finite positive Rf,Rin,C','Find the leaky-integrator transfer.','Feedback impedance',r'Z_f=\frac{R_f}{1+sR_fC},\quad H=-Z_f/R_{in}',r'The feedback resistor and capacitor have summed admittance 1/Rf+sC. | Invert that admittance to obtain Zf. | Applying inverting gain −Zf/Rin gives option C; its DC gain is finite rather than an ideal integrator’s infinity.','Parallel impedances combine as admittances first.')
b.n(r'For the leaky integrator with Rf=100 kΩ and C=10 nF, find its feedback pole in s⁻¹.',-1000,'Parallel RC feedback; linear negative-feedback operation','Find root of 1+sRfC.','Leaky-integrator pole',r'p=-1/(R_fC)',r'The feedback time constant is 10⁵×10⁻⁸=10⁻³ s. | Set 1+s×10⁻³=0. | The pole is −1000 s⁻¹.','The minus sign is required for a decaying passive feedback time constant.',unit='s⁻¹',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'An ideal difference-amplifier network has perfectly matched resistor ratios R2/R1=R4/R3=5 and transfer vo=5(v2−v1). For v2=1.2 V and v1=0.8 V, find vo.',2,'Matched difference transfer explicitly given; differential input 0.4 V','Find the output from the differential component.','Common-mode rejection in a matched subtractor',r'v_o=k(v_2-v_1)',r'The common contribution cancels because the ratios match. | Subtract the input voltages: 1.2−0.8=0.4 V. | Multiply by gain 5 to obtain 2 V.','Use the input order in the specified transfer; swapping them reverses the sign.',unit='V')
b.n(r'An active first-order low-pass has H(s)=2/(1+0.001s). Find |H(j1000)|.',1.4142135623730951,'DC gain 2; corner coefficient 0.001 s; ω=1000 rad/s','Find corner magnitude including nonunit passband gain.','Active-filter magnitude',r'|H(j\omega)|=\frac2{\sqrt{1+(0.001\omega)^2}}',r'The normalized frequency is 0.001×1000=1. | The denominator magnitude is √(1+1)=√2. | Magnitude is 2/√2=√2≈1.414213562.','The corner is 1/√2 times DC gain, not necessarily an absolute gain 0.707.')
b.s(r'Which statements are valid for an ideal inverting summing amplifier in linear operation?',['A','B','D'],['Each input contributes with weight −Rf/Ri','The input currents add at the summing node','The output is independent of input resistor values','Opposite-signed input voltages can cancel at the output'],'Grounded noninverting pin; positive resistances; stable feedback','Select consequences of summing-node KCL.','Weighted superposition',r'v_o=-R_f\sum_i v_i/R_i',r'Zero input-pin current forces the sum of signal currents through feedback. | Each signal is weighted by its individual conductance and feedback resistance. | Properly weighted positive and negative inputs can cancel. Select A,B,D.','Cancellation depends on weighted currents, not only raw input voltages.')
b.s(r'Which assumptions are needed to use v+=v− in the ideal op-amp model?',['A','C'],['The device is operating in its linear unsaturated region','The circuit is necessarily open-loop','Appropriate stable negative feedback constrains a finite output','Positive feedback alone always enforces the equality'],'Ideal infinite-gain limit','Identify the valid application domain of virtual equality.','Limits of ideal constraints',r'v_+-v_-=v_o/A\to0\quad(A\to\infty)',r'A finite unsaturated output allows the differential voltage to tend to zero as A grows. | A properly acting stable negative-feedback loop supplies that operating constraint. | Open-loop or positive-feedback operation does not generally do so. Select A,C.','Do not apply the virtual-short equation to a saturated comparator.')
b.n(r'An op-amp output is hard-clamped at −10 V. Its noninverting input is grounded; the inverting node connects through 10 kΩ to +1 V and through 200 kΩ to the output. Input-pin current is zero. Find the actual inverting-node voltage.',0.47619047619047616,'Saturated output specified; no virtual-short assumption; two resistive branches','Solve the actual saturated summing-node voltage.','Consistency beyond the linear region',r'(v_- -1)/10k+(v_-+10)/200k=0',r'Multiply by 200 kΩ: 20(v−−1)+(v−+10)=0. | This gives 21v−=10 and v−=10/21≈0.476190476 V. | Since v+=0<v−, the negative output direction is consistent; assuming virtual ground here would be wrong.','The ideal input-current constraint survives the clamp model, but virtual equality does not.',difficulty='hard',unit='V')
b.n(r'An ideal inverting integrator obeys dy/dt=−100x. Starting at y=0, x=1 for 2 ms and then x=−2 for 1 ms. Find the final y in volts.',0,'Two constant pulse areas of opposite sign; no saturation','Sum both input areas before integrating.','Pulse-area cancellation',r'\Delta y=-100\sum_k x_k\Delta t_k',r'The first input area is 1×0.002=0.002 V·s. | The second is −2×0.001=−0.002 V·s; total input area is zero. | The final output returns to its initial value, 0 V, despite nonzero intermediate excursion.','Different pulse amplitudes can cancel if their signed areas match.',difficulty='hard',unit='V')

b=Bank('topic-34','control','stability','Stability · Routh, Nyquist & Internal Modes','009');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'How many right-half-plane roots does s³−2s²−s+2 have?',2,'Real cubic polynomial','Count roots with positive real part.','Factoring and pole location',r's^3-2s^2-s+2=(s-2)(s-1)(s+1)',r'Group terms as s²(s−2)−(s−2). | Factor to (s−2)(s−1)(s+1). | Roots 2 and 1 lie in the right half-plane; −1 lies in the left. The count is 2.','Count roots by real part, not by polynomial coefficient signs alone.')
b.n(r'For s⁴+2s³+3s²+4s+K with K>0, find the upper boundary of the strictly stable K interval.',2,'Quartic polynomial; K positive','Find the parameter boundary from the Routh array.','Quartic Routh conditions',r'\text{first column}=1,2,1,4-2K,K',r'The s² first entry is (2×3−4)/2=1, with second entry K. | The s¹ first entry is 4−2K. | Strict positivity requires 0<K<2, so the upper excluded boundary is 2.','Positive coefficients alone are insufficient; the intermediate Routh entries also constrain K.',difficulty='hard')
b.m(r'Which causal ordinary transfer function is BIBO stable?','B',[r'$1/(s^2+1)$',r'$1/(s+2)^2$',r'$1/s$',r'$1/(s-2)$'],'No cancellations; continuous-time causal rational systems','Choose a response with absolutely integrable impulse response.','Strict pole stability',r'\operatorname{Re}p_i<0',r'B has only the repeated pole −2; its impulse response te⁻²ᵗ decays integrably. | A and C have imaginary-axis poles, while D has a right-half-plane pole. | Only B meets the strict BIBO condition.','Repeated poles are allowed in the left half-plane.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'The first column of a valid Routh array is 1,−2,−3,4,5. Assuming no zero-row special case, how many roots lie in the right half-plane?',2,'Five nonzero first-column entries','Count sign changes.','Routh sign-change count',r'N_{RHP}=\#\text{sign changes in first column}',r'The signs are +,−,−,+,+. | Sign changes occur from the first to second and third to fourth entries. | There are 2 right-half-plane roots.','Count sign transitions rather than the number of negative entries.')
b.n(r'For A=[[0,1],[0,0]], x(0)=(0,1)ᵀ, find x1(3).',3,'Nilpotent state matrix; initial second state 1','Find first state and recognize internal growth.','Jordan block at the origin',r'e^{At}=I+At\quad(A^2=0)',r'The equations are xdot2=0 and xdot1=x2. | Therefore x2=1 and x1=t from the stated initial condition. | At t=3, x1=3, demonstrating unbounded growth despite both eigenvalues being zero.','Nonpositive eigenvalue real parts alone do not guarantee bounded dynamics when boundary Jordan blocks occur.',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For a real 2×2 state matrix A, which conditions together are necessary and sufficient for asymptotic stability?',['A','C'],['trace(A)<0','trace(A)>0','det(A)>0','det(A)<0'],'Continuous-time state system with real coefficients','Select the two required strict inequalities.','Second-order Hurwitz criterion',r'\lambda^2-\operatorname{tr}(A)\lambda+\det A=0',r'A real monic quadratic is Hurwitz exactly when its linear and constant coefficients are positive. | Here those coefficients are −trace(A) and det(A). | Thus trace(A)<0 and det(A)>0 are required together: A and C.','Each inequality alone is insufficient; use both for a real second-order system.')
b.n(r'A Nyquist convention defines Z=P+N, with N the net clockwise encirclements of −1. If P=1 and closed-loop stability requires Z=0, find N.',-1,'Specified Nyquist sign convention; one open-loop RHP pole','Compute the necessary net encirclement count.','Nyquist accounting convention',r'Z=P+N',r'Substitute target Z=0 and open-loop P=1. | Rearranging gives N=0−1. | N=−1 means one net counterclockwise encirclement under the convention explicitly stated.','Encirclement sign conventions vary; always use the stated one.')
b.m(r'Which statement about a pole-zero cancellation at a positive real pole is safest for internal stability?','D',['Cancellation always stabilizes every state','Any canceled mode becomes left-half-plane','Output cancellation proves all internal modes decay','A measured transfer can hide a growing internal mode'],'Finite-dimensional realization may contain unobservable/uncontrollable states','Identify what transfer cancellation does not guarantee.','Internal versus input-output stability',r'\dot x=Ax\quad\text{is governed by all eigenvalues of }A',r'An unstable state may not appear in a particular measured transfer when it is unobservable or not excited through that input. | Algebraic transfer cancellation does not physically move that eigenvalue. | It can therefore hide a growing internal mode, option D.','A simplified transfer should not replace an internal-state stability audit.')
b.n(r'For H(s)=1/(s²+1) and input sin t applied from t=0 with zero state, write the output as y(t)=a sin t+b t cos t. Find the magnitude |b|.',0.5,'Undamped resonant transfer; unit sine input; zero initial state','Find secular growth coefficient.','Resonance and BIBO failure',r'Y(s)=1/(s^2+1)^2',r'The inverse transform is y(t)=(sin t−t cos t)/2 for t≥0. | The term proportional to t cos t grows without a constant bound. | Its coefficient is −1/2 and its magnitude is 0.5, demonstrating failure of BIBO stability.','A bounded sinusoidal input can cause resonance at an imaginary-axis pole.')
b.n(r'A characteristic polynomial is s³+s²+s+10. Find its number of right-half-plane roots.',2,'All coefficients positive; cubic Routh array','Test whether coefficient positivity suffices.','Routh intermediate entry',r'\text{first column}=1,1,-9,10',r'The s¹ entry is (1×1−10)/1=−9. | The first-column signs are +,+,−,+. | There are 2 sign changes and therefore 2 right-half-plane roots despite positive polynomial coefficients.','Positive coefficients are necessary in a monic Hurwitz polynomial but not sufficient for degree three or higher.')
b.s(r'Which statements about stable continuous-time LTI dynamics are correct?',['A','B','D'],['Polynomial factors multiplying a strictly decaying exponential still tend to zero','A pole repeated at −1 can remain asymptotically stable','Every repeated pole is unstable','A simple pole at zero does not satisfy strict asymptotic stability'],'Finite-dimensional ordinary LTI modes','Select valid modal-decay facts.','Exponential dominance',r't^m e^{-at}\to0\quad(a>0)',r'Exponential decay dominates every finite polynomial factor. | Thus repeated negative-real-part poles are allowed in a stable system. | A zero pole leaves a nondecaying mode, so it is outside strict asymptotic stability. Select A,B,D.','Multiplicity must be considered together with pole location.')
b.n(r'For s²+2as+9 with a real, find the boundary value of a separating the strictly stable region a>0 from non-Hurwitz cases.',0,'Monic real second-order polynomial; fixed positive constant 9','Find where the linear coefficient ceases to be strictly positive.','Second-order stability boundary',r'2a>0,\quad9>0',r'Hurwitz stability requires positive linear and constant coefficients. | Only the linear coefficient depends on a. | The boundary is a=0, giving imaginary-axis roots ±3j; strict stability requires a>0.','The boundary itself is not included in the strictly stable region.',difficulty='easy')

b=Bank('topic-41','communications','information-theory','Information Theory · Codes, Uncertainty & Limits','009');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A binary erasure channel outputs the correct input bit with probability 0.75 and an explicit erasure symbol with probability 0.25. Find its capacity in bits/use.',0.75,'Memoryless erasure probability 0.25; erasures identifiable; binary input','Find information retained per use at optimal input.','Erasure-channel capacity',r'C=(1-\epsilon)\log_22',r'With a uniform input, a successful delivery reveals one full bit. | An erasure reveals none about that bit and occurs with probability 0.25. | The retained average and capacity are (1−0.25)×1=0.75 bits/use.','An erasure is distinguishable from a flipped bit, so its capacity law differs from a binary symmetric channel.',unit='bits/use')
b.n(r'Two independent fair input bits X1,X2 are mapped deterministically to Y=X1 XOR X2. Find I((X1,X2);Y) in bits.',1,'Four equiprobable input pairs; parity output','Find mutual information through a many-to-one mapping.','Deterministic mapping information',r'I(X;Y)=H(Y)-H(Y|X)',r'Parity is equally likely 0 or 1, so H(Y)=1 bit. | It is fixed once both input bits are known, so H(Y|X1,X2)=0. | Mutual information is 1 bit even though the pair contains 2 bits.','A deterministic map need not preserve all input information when it is not one-to-one.',unit='bits')
b.n(r'Four equiprobable source symbols use binary prefix labels 0,10,110,111. Find the mean code length in bits/symbol.',2.25,'Four codewords with lengths 1,2,3,3; probability 1/4 each','Average codeword lengths.','Mean source-code length',r'\bar L=\sum_i p_i\ell_i',r'The prefix labels have the stated four lengths. | Sum the lengths to get 9 bits across four equally likely symbols. | The mean is 9/4=2.25 bits/symbol, above entropy 2.','Prefix freedom does not imply a code is optimal for every probability distribution.',unit='bits/symbol',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'Which binary code-length lists can satisfy the Kraft inequality for a prefix code?',['A','C','D'],['1,2,2','1,1,2','2,2,2,2','1,3,3'],'Finite binary code; only codeword lengths specified','Select lists with Σ2⁻ˡ≤1.','Kraft bound',r'\sum_i2^{-\ell_i}\le1',r'A sums to 1/2+1/4+1/4=1, while B sums to 1.25 and is impossible. | C sums to 4×1/4=1; D sums to 1/2+1/8+1/8=0.75. | Thus A,C,D can be realized as binary prefix-code lengths.','A sum below 1 is allowed; it indicates unused coding-tree space.')
b.m(r'Which listed binary label set is prefix-free?','A',['{0,10,11}','{0,01,11}','{1,10,00}','{00,001,11}'],'No complete codeword may be the prefix of a different codeword','Check all pairs for the prefix property.','Instantaneous source coding',r'c_i\not\prec c_j\quad(i\ne j)',r'In A, the word 0 is not a prefix of the words starting with 1, and the two length-2 words differ. | B contains 0 as prefix of 01; C contains 1 as prefix of 10; D contains 00 as prefix of 001. | Only A is prefix-free.','Check complete-codeword prefixes, not merely whether first bits are repeated.')
b.n(r'A Huffman code is designed for probabilities 0.4,0.3,0.2,0.1. Find its optimal average binary length in bits/symbol.',1.9,'Four source probabilities; optimal binary prefix tree sought','Run smallest-weight merges.','Huffman merging',r'\bar L_{Huffman}=\text{sum of merge weights}',r'Merge 0.1+0.2=0.3, then the two 0.3 nodes to get 0.6. | Merge 0.4+0.6=1.0. The merge weights sum to 0.3+0.6+1=1.9. | This equals the mean length of depths 1,2,3,3 weighted by 0.4,0.3,0.2,0.1.','Combine the smallest current nodes at each stage, including newly formed nodes.',unit='bits/symbol',difficulty='hard')
b.m(r'For an optimal binary prefix code of a finite source with at least two positive-probability symbols and entropy H, the usual length bound is:','C',[r'$\bar L<H$ always',r'$\bar L=H+1$ always',r'$H\le\bar L<H+1$',r'$\bar L\ge H+2$'],'Binary prefix coding; finite source; nondegenerate alphabet','Identify the entropy coding bound.','Source-code lower/upper bounds',r'H\le\bar L_{opt}<H+1',r'Kraft-based coding inequalities make entropy a lower bound on expected prefix length. | A Shannon length construction achieves an average strictly below H+1 for the stated positive-probability finite source. | An optimal code cannot be worse than that construction, giving option C.','The bound does not say an integer-length single-symbol code must attain entropy exactly.')
b.n(r'X,Y are independent fair bits with joint probabilities 1/4 in every cell. Find I(X;Y) in bits.',0,'Independent binary variables; uniform joint table','Evaluate mutual information.','Independence information',r'I(X;Y)=\sum_{x,y}p(x,y)\log_2\frac{p(x,y)}{p(x)p(y)}',r'Each marginal probability is 1/2. | In every cell the ratio is (1/4)/[(1/2)(1/2)]=1. | Every log term is zero, so I(X;Y)=0 bits.','Shared binary alphabets do not imply statistical dependence.',unit='bits',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'X is a fair bit and Y is always zero, independent of X. Find H(X|Y) in bits.',1,'Y is constant; X has entropy 1','Find remaining uncertainty after observing Y.','Uninformative observation',r'H(X|Y=0)=H(X)',r'The observation Y=0 occurs with certainty for either input value. | It therefore leaves the posterior distribution of X fair. | The conditional entropy remains 1 bit.','A constant output has zero entropy but does not force the input’s conditional entropy to zero.',unit='bits')
b.n(r'Let X be a fair bit and let Z=(X,X,X,X), four exact copies. Find H(Z) in bits.',1,'Perfectly correlated four-bit vector; only 0000 and 1111 possible','Find joint uncertainty.','Joint entropy with dependence',r'H(Z)=H(X)\quad\text{for an injective deterministic copy map}',r'Only two vectors are possible, each with probability 1/2. | Their entropy is −2×(1/2)log₂(1/2). | H(Z)=1 bit rather than four, because the components are not independent.','Entropy additivity requires independence, not merely a vector of four bit positions.',unit='bits')
b.n(r'For an AWGN channel with fixed received signal power P and white-noise spectral coefficient N0, C(B)=B log₂(1+P/(N0B)). Find lim as B→∞ of C(B)/(P/N0).',1.4426950408889634,'Fixed P,N0; bandwidth B increases; stated real baseband capacity convention','Find the normalized infinite-bandwidth limit.','Power-limited capacity asymptote',r'\lim_{u\to0}\frac{\ln(1+u)}u=1',r'Let u=P/(N0B), so the normalized expression is log₂(1+u)/u. | As B→∞, u→0 and ln(1+u)/u→1. | Converting logarithm base gives limit 1/ln2≈1.442695041.','Holding power fixed makes SNR decrease as bandwidth grows; it is not a fixed-SNR limit.',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For finite discrete random variables with their ordinary Shannon entropies, which inequalities are always valid?',['A','B','D'],['H(X)≥0','I(X;Y)≤H(X)','H(X,Y)=H(X)+H(Y) for every joint distribution','H(X)≤log₂M when X has at most M possible values'],'Finite alphabets; all logs base 2','Select universal entropy bounds.','Information bounds',r'I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)',r'Nonnegative probabilities and conditional uncertainty give H≥0 and I≤H(X). | Entropy is maximized by a uniform distribution over the available M values, yielding log₂M. | Joint entropy is additive only under independence, so C is false. Select A,B,D.','Do not apply the independence equality to arbitrary correlated variables.')

b=Bank('topic-46','electromagnetics','transmission-lines','Transmission Lines · Waves, Loss & Networks','009');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A lossless line has per-unit-length L′=250 nH/m and C′=100 pF/m. Find its characteristic impedance in ohms.',50,'Lossless distributed line; L′=250×10⁻⁹ H/m; C′=100×10⁻¹² F/m','Find √(L′/C′).','Distributed-line impedance',r'Z_0=\sqrt{L^\prime/C^\prime}',r'The ratio is (250×10⁻⁹)/(100×10⁻¹²)=2500 Ω². | Take its positive square root. | Z0=50 Ω.','The impedance uses a ratio, whereas propagation speed uses the product.',unit='Ω')
b.n(r'For the same stated lossless parameters L′=250 nH/m and C′=100 pF/m, find propagation speed in units of 10⁸ m/s.',2,'Distributed inductance and capacitance; no losses','Find reciprocal √(L′C′).','Telegrapher wave speed',r'v_p=1/\sqrt{L^\prime C^\prime}',r'The product is 2.5×10⁻¹⁷ s²/m². | Its square root is 5×10⁻⁹ s/m. | Speed is 2×10⁸ m/s, so enter 2 in requested units.','The units of the product’s square root are delay per length.',unit='×10⁸ m/s')
b.n(r'A lossless short-circuited line has Z0=50 Ω and length λ/8. Find the imaginary part of Zin in ohms.',50,'Short termination; βl=π/4','Find Im(jZ0tanβl).','Shorted-stub impedance',r'Z_{in}=jZ_0\tan(\beta l)',r'At λ/8 the electrical length is π/4. | tan(π/4)=1, giving Zin=j50 Ω. | Its imaginary part is +50 Ω, inductive under the stated phasor convention.','A shorted stub need not look like a short away from zero or half-wavelength lengths.',unit='Ω')
b.n(r'A lossless open-circuited line has Z0=50 Ω and length λ/8. Find the imaginary part of Zin in ohms.',-50,'Open termination; βl=π/4','Find the open-stub reactance sign.','Open-stub impedance',r'Z_{in}=-jZ_0\cot(\beta l)',r'The open termination produces the cotangent form. | cot(π/4)=1, so Zin=−j50 Ω. | The imaginary part is −50 Ω, capacitive.','Open and shorted stubs have different reactance functions and signs.',unit='Ω')
b.n(r'A line has |Γ|=0.1 at the load. Find return loss −20log₁₀|Γ| in dB.',20,'Reflection amplitude 0.1; positive return-loss convention','Convert reflection coefficient to return loss.','Mismatch logarithmic measure',r'RL=-20\log_{10}|\Gamma|',r'log₁₀0.1=−1. | Multiply by −20. | Return loss is 20 dB; higher positive return loss means smaller reflection.','Return loss is positive under this convention, while 20log|Γ| is negative.',unit='dB',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A lossless line has normalized load zL=1+j relative to real Z0. Find the imaginary part of ΓL.',0.4,'Normalized complex load 1+j','Compute (zL−1)/(zL+1).','Smith-plane reflection mapping',r'\Gamma=(z-1)/(z+1)',r'The ratio is j/(2+j). | Multiply numerator and denominator by 2−j to obtain (1+2j)/5. | The imaginary part is 2/5=0.4, while the real part is 0.2.','Normalize to the reference impedance before using the normalized Smith-chart map.')
b.m(r'For an ideal passive resistive load ZL>0 on a lossless real-Z0 line, its reflection coefficient lies:','B',['Outside the unit circle necessarily','On the real axis strictly between −1 and +1','Only at +1','Only on the imaginary axis'],'Finite strictly positive real ZL and Z0','Locate its Smith-plane point.','Passive-resistance reflection locus',r'\Gamma=(R_L-Z_0)/(R_L+Z_0)',r'The ratio of real quantities is real. | Since both resistances are positive, the numerator magnitude is strictly less than the denominator. | Thus −1<Γ<1 on the real axis, option B.','The endpoints correspond to zero or infinite resistance limits, excluded here.')
b.n(r'A forward traveling voltage wave decays as e⁻ᵅˡ on a matched lossy line. If α=0.1 Np/m and l=10 m, find output/input power ratio.',0.1353352832366127,'Matched line; equal real power-wave normalization; voltage attenuation e⁻¹','Square the amplitude attenuation.','Voltage versus power attenuation',r'P_{out}/P_{in}=e^{-2\alpha l}',r'The voltage amplitude attenuation is e⁻¹. | Power is proportional to its squared magnitude in the same normalization. | The power ratio is e⁻²≈0.1353352832.','A nepers-per-meter amplitude attenuation doubles its exponent when converted to power.')
b.n(r'Adjacent voltage maxima on a lossless standing-wave line are measured 0.4 m apart. Find the guided wavelength in meters.',0.8,'Consecutive standing-wave maxima spacing 0.4 m','Find twice the measured spacing.','Standing-wave spatial periodicity',r'\Delta l_{max}=\lambda/2',r'The standing-wave envelope repeats each half wavelength. | The measured adjacent-maxima separation is 0.4 m. | Therefore λ=2×0.4=0.8 m.','Neighboring maximum and minimum are λ/4 apart; two maxima are λ/2 apart.',unit='m')
b.s(r'For a reciprocal two-port using identical real reference impedances, which statements hold?',['A','C'],['S12=S21','S11=S22 follows from reciprocity alone','Reciprocity constrains transmission symmetry','Every reciprocal two-port is lossless'],'Normalized linear two-port scattering parameters','Select reciprocal-network consequences.','Scattering reciprocity',r'S^T=S',r'Reciprocity gives equality of forward and reverse transmission parameters under the stated normalization. | It does not force identical input/output reflection coefficients; that requires additional symmetry. | A reciprocal resistor network can dissipate power, so losslessness is not automatic. Select A,C.','Reciprocal, symmetric and lossless are distinct properties.')
b.n(r'On a lossless line, the reflection coefficient at distance l toward the generator is Γin=ΓL exp(−j2βl). For ΓL=1 and l=λ/8, find Im Γin.',-1,'Reference moves toward generator; specified phase relation; ΓL=1','Evaluate the rotated reflection coefficient.','Round-trip reflection phase',r'2\beta l=2(2\pi/\lambda)(\lambda/8)=\pi/2',r'The round-trip phase rotation is −π/2. | Multiplying ΓL=1 by e⁻ʲπᐟ² yields −j. | The imaginary part is −1.','The reflection coefficient accumulates twice the one-way propagation phase.',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'Which statements about an ideal matched lossless line section are true?',['A','B','D'],['Its input impedance equals Z0 for any length when terminated in Z0','Its transmitted-wave magnitude is unchanged along the section','It introduces zero phase delay at every frequency regardless of length','The line can introduce propagation delay without reflecting a wave'],'Uniform lossless line; matched termination','Select matching versus propagation effects.','Matched traveling waves',r'\Gamma_L=0,\quad V^+(l)=V^+(0)e^{-j\beta l}',r'A matched load removes the backward wave, leaving the same V/I ratio Z0 everywhere. | Losslessness preserves traveling-wave magnitude. | The phase factor can still produce delay, so phase need not be zero. Select A,B,D.','Absence of reflection is not absence of propagation time.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-50','aptitude','quantitative-aptitude','Quantitative Aptitude · Ratios, Data & Counting','009');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Two student groups have sizes 10 and 30 and mean scores 60 and 80 respectively. Find their combined mean score.',75,'Group sizes 10,30; group means 60,80','Find weighted average by total score.','Weighted mean',r'\bar x=(n_1\bar x_1+n_2\bar x_2)/(n_1+n_2)',r'The groups contribute total scores 600 and 2400. | Total score is 3000 over 40 students. | The combined mean is 3000/40=75.','The unweighted mean of group means is valid only for equal group sizes.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A machine produces 120 items on Monday and 180 on Tuesday. Defect rates are 5% and 10% respectively. Find the combined defect percentage.',8,'Daily counts and corresponding defect fractions','Find total defects divided by total output.','Weighted rate from data',r'p_{total}=\frac{N_1p_1+N_2p_2}{N_1+N_2}',r'Defects are 120×0.05=6 and 180×0.10=18. | The total is 24 defective items out of 300. | The fraction is 24/300=0.08, or 8%.','Averaging 5% and 10% without their output weights gives the wrong rate.',unit='%')
b.n(r'If a:b=2:3 and b:c=4:5, find a/c.',0.5333333333333333,'Two linked ratios with common b','Eliminate the shared variable.','Ratio chaining',r'a/c=(a/b)(b/c)',r'The shared b cancels when ratios multiply. | (2/3)(4/5)=8/15. | The required ratio is approximately 0.5333333333.','Match or cancel the common variable before comparing a and c.')
b.n(r'Find the sum 1+2+…+50.',1275,'Arithmetic sequence of first 50 positive integers','Find the finite series sum.','Arithmetic-series sum',r'S_n=n(n+1)/2',r'Pair first and last terms to get 51 per pair. | There are 25 such pairs. | The sum is 25×51=1275.','Count the number of terms rather than using only the largest value.',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'For positive x, log₂x=5 implies x equals:','D',['10','25','64','32'],'Base-2 logarithm; positive domain','Invert the logarithm.','Exponent–log inverse',r'\log_2x=5\iff x=2^5',r'A logarithm reports the exponent needed for its base. | Here x is 2 raised to the fifth power. | 2⁵=32, so option D is correct.','The log base is 2, not e or 10.')
b.n(r'How many 3-member committees can be formed from 6 distinct people if order within the committee does not matter?',20,'Six available people; choose three; no ordering roles','Count combinations.','Unordered selection',r'\binom63=6!/(3!3!)',r'Ordered selections would count 6×5×4=120. | Each committee appears 3!=6 times under those orderings. | Divide to get 120/6=20 committees.','Use permutations only when member roles or order matter.')
b.n(r'A box has 4 good and 1 defective device. Two are chosen without replacement. Find the probability of at least one defective device.',0.4,'Five distinct devices; one defective; two sampled','Use complement or direct combinations.','Sampling without replacement',r'P(\ge1D)=1-\binom42/\binom52',r'There are C(5,2)=10 unordered samples. | Six samples use only the four good devices. | The complement probability is 1−6/10=0.4.','Do not treat two draws without replacement as independent.')
b.n(r'A square and a circle have equal perimeter. Find the ratio of the square’s area to the circle’s area.',0.7853981633974483,'Common perimeter P>0','Express both areas using the common perimeter.','Isoperimetric comparison',r'A_{sq}=P^2/16,\quad A_{circ}=P^2/(4\pi)',r'The square side is P/4, giving area P²/16. | The circle radius is P/(2π), giving area P²/(4π). | Their ratio is π/4≈0.7853981634.','Equal perimeter does not imply equal area.')
b.s(r'For nonnegative data values, which transformations necessarily produce the stated effect?',['A','C'],['Adding 5 to every value adds 5 to the mean','Adding 5 to every value adds 5 to the standard deviation','Multiplying every value by 2 multiplies standard deviation by 2','Multiplying every value by 2 multiplies variance by 2'],'Finite data set with population variance convention','Select valid location and scale rules.','Statistics under affine transformations',r'E[aX+b]=aE[X]+b,\quad Var(aX+b)=a^2Var(X)',r'A constant shift moves the mean but leaves all centered deviations unchanged. | A factor 2 doubles deviations and standard deviation, while quadrupling variance. | Therefore A,C hold.','Variance scales quadratically, whereas standard deviation scales linearly.')
b.n(r'A train 150 m long passes a fixed point in 10 s at constant speed. Find its speed in km/h.',54,'Train length 150 m; full pass time 10 s; point observer','Convert length/time into km/h.','Speed unit conversion',r'v=(150/10)\cdot3.6',r'The entire train must advance its own length past the point. | Speed is 150/10=15 m/s. | Multiply by 3.6 to obtain 54 km/h.','For passing a platform, its length would also matter; the observer here is a fixed point.',unit='km/h')
b.n(r'A positive number is selected uniformly from integers 1 to 30. Find the probability it is divisible by 2 or by 3.',0.6666666666666666,'Uniform finite integer set; union of two divisibility events','Count the union without double-counting multiples of 6.','Divisibility inclusion–exclusion',r'N=\lfloor30/2\rfloor+\lfloor30/3\rfloor-\lfloor30/6\rfloor',r'There are 15 multiples of 2 and 10 multiples of 3. | Their overlap contains 5 multiples of 6. | Union count is 15+10−5=20, so probability is 20/30=2/3.','Inclusive “or” includes numbers satisfying both conditions.',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'A population has mean 10 and population variance 4. Which statements follow for the transformed variable Y=3X−2?',['A','B','D'],['Mean(Y)=28','Variance(Y)=36','Standard deviation(Y)=12','Standard deviation(Y)=6'],'Affine transformation with slope 3','Propagate mean, variance and standard deviation.','Affine moment transformation',r'\mu_Y=3\mu_X-2,\quad\sigma_Y^2=9\sigma_X^2',r'The mean is 3×10−2=28. | Variance is 9×4=36; taking its square root gives standard deviation 6. | Thus A,B,D hold, while 12 incorrectly scales the variance as if it were standard deviation.','A constant offset affects the mean but not centered variation.',difficulty='hard')
save_batch(banks,'batch-009')
